Negative control for the route-collision guard the registrar adds (amendment 2).

Nuxt's own duplicate check runs before `pages:extend` and only warns — never at
all for hook-pushed routes — so the registrar's `pages:resolved` guard is the
only thing standing between a scenario directory and a page that already owns
its name. This mutant puts the guard to sleep without removing it: a duplicate
must now appear THREE times to count, so a colliding PAIR — the only shape the
defect ever takes — walks straight through and the build ships two router
records over one url, exactly as silently as before the guard existed.

Off-by-one rather than an amputation on purpose: a deleted guard is visible in
any diff, while a threshold nobody reads is what actually survives review.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Reds (module lane, measured 2026-08-11 — 2 failed, 16 passed):
  scenario-routes.spec.ts   "hard-fails when a page already owns a scenario's
                              name"
                            "names the offending route rather than failing
                              anonymously"

The other two collision cases stay GREEN — "lets an unrelated page through" and
"passes on the shipped tree" both assert the guard does NOT fire, and a sleeping
guard satisfies them perfectly. That asymmetry is the control's whole point: a
suite carrying only the negative cases would grade this mutant as a pass.

